# Hooks & State Management

## Hook Rules

- Call hooks only at the **top level** — never inside loops, conditions, or nested functions.
- Extract reusable stateful logic into **custom hooks** prefixed with `use` (e.g., `useAuth`, `usePagination`).
- Keep each custom hook focused on a single concern.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — logic mixed into component
function ProductList() {
  const [products, setProducts] = useState([]);
  useEffect(() => { fetch('/api/products').then(...) }, []);
  ...
}

// ✅ GOOD — extracted custom hook
function useProducts() {
  const [products, setProducts] = useState<Product[]>([]);
  useEffect(() => { ... }, []);
  return products;
}
function ProductList() {
  const products = useProducts();
  ...
}
```

## State Management

- Prefer **local state** (`useState`) unless state is shared across sibling/parent components.
- Lift state to the **nearest common ancestor** before reaching for global state.
- Use **Context + `useReducer`** for moderately complex shared state; prefer a dedicated library (Zustand, Jotai) for app-wide state.
- Derive values from existing state instead of duplicating them in separate state variables.
